Learning GitHub Actions – Day 6: Runners
Continuing my GitHub Actions learning journey as part of my Cloud & DevOps roadmap!

📌 What are Runners in GitHub Actions?
Runners are servers or machines that execute the jobs defined in a GitHub Actions workflow.
Whenever a workflow is triggered, GitHub assigns the job to an available runner, which provides the environment required to execute commands, build applications, run tests, and perform deployments.
There are two main types of runners:
GitHub-hosted runners: Machines managed by GitHub with preconfigured environments.
Self-hosted runners: Machines that we configure and manage ourselves, such as an EC2 instance or an on-premises server.

🛠️ Practical Example:

name: Runners Demo  

on:
   push:
      branches:       
             - main  

 jobs:
    demo:     
        runs-on: ubuntu-latest     
        steps:
           - name: Display Runner Information
              run: |
                    echo "Running on GitHub Actions Runner"
                    echo "Operating System:"
                     uname -a        

          - name: Check Working Directory
             run: pwd

🔍 How it works:
runs-on: Specifies which runner environment to use.
ubuntu-latest: Uses a GitHub-hosted Ubuntu runner.
run: Executes commands on the selected runner.
The runner executes all steps within the assigned job.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Runners provide the execution environment for GitHub Actions jobs. Understanding GitHub-hosted and self-hosted runners helps us choose the right infrastructure for CI/CD automation.
